.TH "PADS" "1" "2025\-02\-26" "pads 0.0.5" "Comprehensive Documentation"
.SH NAME
PADS \- Personal Assistant and Deployment System
.SH SYNOPSIS
<cmd> \-[<module>] \-[<argument>] \-[<option>] [name\&...]
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-S \-[b,u,i,l,d,r:,m,s,h,v] [name\&...]
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-P \-[p:,f:,g:,h,v] \-[m:,s:,d\-h] <name>
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-R \-[s:,v,h] <name\&...>
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-T [\-h|\-v]
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-x
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-h
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
PADS is a general purpose programming language designed to automate
tasks, and run complex scripts in a for‐ mat that only involves a few
flags, and options.
The first flag in the syntax is made to access logic from modules which
may have their own complex logic.
The second flag in the syntax is made to access options that may be
available in those modules.
PADS also has robust error checking; so you can be sure not to enter the
wrong flags.
Also see: info pads for the comprehensive guide.
This may become out of date if I forget to up‐ date it.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS \f[B]\-S\f[R] \-[b,u,i,l,d,r:,m,s,h,v] [name\&...]
Run automated system scripts and exit.
The logic for this module is intended to interact with the OS itself.
The options are as follows:
.TP
\f[B]\-b\f[R]
Backup your system to {set a location}
.TP
\f[B]\-u\f[R] [name\&...]
Update your machine.
If given a name it will attempt to install packages.
.TP
\f[B]\-i\f[R]
Update pads info page.
Docs are at: pads/docs/pads.texi
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R]
List packages installed on this machine.
.TP
\f[B]\-d\f[R]
Update pads\[cq] man page.
Docs are at: pads/docs/pads.1.
.TP
\f[B]\-r\f[R] <name\&...>
Remove supplied packages
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R]
Set up Mariadb
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
Show help
.SS \f[B]\-P\f[R] \-[p:|f:|g:|h|v] \-[<m|s|d>] <name>
Run modules parsed in another language and exit.
This module is intended to run full programs.
Usually something other than Bash.
The m flag is make, the s flag is start, the d flag is delete the
project.
.TP
\f[B]\-p\f[R] \-[<m|s|d>] 
For managing a Python Project or Module.
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R] \-[<m|s|d>] 
For managing a Flask Project or Module.
.TP
\f[B]\-g\f[R] \-[<m|s|d>] 
For managing a Pygames Project or Module.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
Show help for the P module and exit
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R]
Show version for the \-p module and exit
.SS \f[B]\-R\f[R] [\-s|\-h|\-v]
Used to run any random script within the r_opts.sh file.
This flag is good for testing.
.TP
\f[B]\-s\f[R] [<name>]
Run the given script.
Good for scripts that dont fit into a module.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
Show help for the R module.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R]
Show version for the R module.
.SS \f[B]\-T\f[R] \-[arg]
For tests scripts
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R]
Show help for PADS and exit.
.TP
\f[B]\-v\f[R]
Show version for PADS and exit
.SH EXAMPLES
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-Ph
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Show project help and exit
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-Ppm brain; \f[B]pads\f[R] \-Pps brain
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Make then start a python project named \[lq]brain\[rq].
This will create a virtual env for the project as well.
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-Pfm brain; \f[B]pads\f[R] \-Pfs brain
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Make a flask project named \[lq]brain\[rq].
This will create a virtual env for the project as well.
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-Ppd brain
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Delete the project named \[lq]brain\[rq].
It will ask confirmation as well.
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-Su
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Update your machine.
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-Su neofetch \&...
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Install neofetch to your machine.
.PP
\f[B]pads\f[R] \-Sb
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Back up your machine locally.
